  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records point in the relevant direction but do not disclose the later result. R3 establishes that a closely related Al-Co-Cr-Fe-Ni alloy undergoes consequential decomposition during a 600 °C anneal. R2 and R7 connect chemical ordering with mechanical instability or strengthening and show that ordering effects weaken at elevated temperature. R10 is especially relevant because it links FCC/B2 interface structure and interface stress concentration to ductility in a closely related dual-phase Al-containing HEA. R17 and R18 add evidence for thermally induced precipitation, ordering, and a change to recovery/recrystallization mechanisms near 700 °C. Nevertheless, none identifies enhanced L12 ordering near Ni3Al stoichiometry, jagged FCC/B2 boundaries, elevated APBE and restricted cross-slip, or the specific 600 °C minimum followed by 750 °C ductility recovery. Combining the records could motivate temperature-dependent aging studies of ordering and interfaces, but predicting the specific ductile-brittle-ductile transition would require a substantial new idea."
